+# Sources for the embeddable widgets and the /embed/ gallery
+
+Built on 2026-10-07 on the branch `feature/embed-widgets` (from `main` 9955550). Code: `site/embed-models.mjs` (the pure parts: options, snippet, loader, feed to view, the computed sky), `site/embed-widgets.mjs` (the five widget pages, their drawing and page loop), `site/embed.mjs` (the gallery and the writer that `site/build.mjs` calls). Tests: `test/embed.test.js` (unit), `e2e-embed.mjs` (browser, `npm run e2e:embed`). Anything marked NOT CONFIRMED has not been checked.
+
+## What was built and where it lives
+- `/embed/` is an ordinary content page (`site/embed.mjs`, `galleryPage`), built by `npm run site` like the guides: in `sitemap.xml` and `llms.txt` (a fixed note, `EMBED_NOTE` in `site/llms.mjs`), indexable unless `SITE_NOINDEX=1`, linked from the home page's live block ("live widgets for your own website", `site/home-text.mjs`).
+- `/embed/earthquakes/`, `/embed/aurora/`, `/embed/tropical-storms/`, `/embed/wildfires/`, `/embed/tonights-sky/` are the iframe documents (`widgetHtml`), written by `writeEmbed` from `site/build.mjs` (one import and one call, nothing else changed there). Each is `noindex`, has no canonical link, and is in no sitemap and not in `llms.txt` (the site build's sitemap is made from the page list, and the widget files are not in it; a test checks both files).
+- They read the same files as the home page strip and the live pages: `live/manifest.json` (fetched with `no-store`) and the versioned feed files it names, relative to the widget (`../../live/`), the same contract as `site/home-strip.mjs`. The server needs no change: `hosting/lib.php` and `pull.php` copy only `live/` and the live pages, and the widget pages arrive with every deploy because the site build writes them.
+
+## Every statement of fact on the gallery, and where it comes from
+| Statement on `/embed/` | Source |
+| --- | --- |
+| The earthquake widget reads the U.S. Geological Survey's feed of magnitude 2.5 and above and shows the 24 hours up to the feed's own time | `pipeline/config.py` (feed `quakes`, `summary/2.5_week.geojson`); the window is `summariseQuakes` in `site/hazard.mjs`; `test/embed.test.js` compares the widget's count and largest quake with it on the fixture |
+| The past hour of the feed is red, the past six hours yellow; dots sized by magnitude, rings larger and slower for larger quakes | OURS, `drawQuakes` in `site/embed-widgets.mjs` |
+| The aurora widget shows the latest planetary Kp index from NOAA SWPC, and the bars the recent three-hour periods | `pipeline/config.py` (feed `kp`, `noaa-planetary-k-index.json`); "Kp now" is the newest period with a value whose tag is not after now, as `summariseKp` and the home strip (`kpModel`, checked against `summariseKp` in the tests). The file held 16 periods (48 hours) in the fixture of 5 October 2026 (`test/fixtures/hazards/live-20261005/kp/`); the widget shows at most 24 and labels the span it has |
+| NOAA's geomagnetic storm levels G1 to G5 start at Kp 5 | `src/scales.js` (`gLevelForKp`), checked against `raw3/noaa-scales-explanation.txt` by `test/scales.test.js`; `wG` is compared with `gLevelForKp` for every third of a Kp step |
+| The storm widget shows the active storms in NHC's list for the Atlantic, Eastern Pacific and Central Pacific, with NHC's forecast track, wind in knots and km/h and the Saffir-Simpson category | `pipeline/config.py` (feed `storms`, `CurrentStorms.json`), `docs/hazard-sources.md` (NHC section), the basins as `NHC_BASINS` in `site/pages-hazard.mjs`; `wCat` is compared with `categoryForKnots` (`src/scales.js`, checked against `raw3/nhc-sshws.txt`) from 0 to 200 knots |
+| The fire widget shows every quarter-degree cell with a detection in NASA FIRMS's 24 hour files for the VIIRS instruments; a detection is heat seen by a satellite, not a confirmed fire | `docs/hazard-sources.md` (FIRMS section), `pipeline/hazards.py` (the cell file), `summariseFires` (same checks; compared in the tests) |
+| The sky widget covers six cities (Pune, New York, London, Tromsø, Tokyo, Sydney), the Moon with its phase and Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn, from sunset to sunrise, with MET Norway's hourly cloud forecast | `SKY_CITY_IDS` and `public/cities.json` (`site/sky-data.mjs`), the clouds feed (`pipeline/config.py`), the sunset and sunrise convention of `nightWindow` in `site/sky.mjs` |
+| The Moon and planet positions are worked out in the visitor's browser with a short set of formulas checked against astronomy-engine | `skyPos` in `site/embed-models.mjs` (Paul Schlyter's low-precision method, written from memory and then checked; it is not copied from a page read for this work). `test/embed.test.js` compares it with astronomy-engine (`Equator` of date with aberration, topocentric, `Horizon` without refraction) at 360 times from 2024 to 2030 over the six cities: worst found when the test was written, Moon 0.095 degrees, Sun 0.015, Mercury 0.021, Venus 0.034, Mars 0.016, Jupiter 0.019, Saturn 0.025, lit fraction 0.0018; the test requires under 0.2 degrees for the Moon, under 0.1 for the rest and under 0.01 for the lit fraction. `skyNight` agrees with `nightWindow` within 2 minutes and the Moon's rises and sets with `riseSetBetween` within 3 minutes on five dates in 2026 for each city where the night is ordinary |
+| "Out of date after" limits: 3, 8, 12 and 8 hours, and 6 hours for the cloud forecast | `MAX_AGE_HOURS` in `site/hazard.mjs` and `SKY_MAX_AGE_HOURS` in `site/sky.mjs`, imported by `WIDGETS`, so the gallery cannot differ from the live pages |
+| A widget looks for new data every five minutes | `pollSec: 300` in the widget's constants (`script` in `site/embed-widgets.mjs`); the manifest's own `pollSec` is also 300 (`test/fixtures/live-manifest.json`) |
+| No cookies, no browser storage, no analytics or tracking, no fonts or scripts from other sites; a content security policy that allows requests to this site only | The widget code; `test/embed.test.js` checks the policy tag and that no storage, cookie or beacon call is in the page; `e2e-embed.mjs` checks that no request leaves the site at three sizes for every widget |
+| The animation stops with reduced motion, out of view and for out-of-date data | `wRun` (`still()`, the IntersectionObserver); checked in `e2e-embed.mjs` with Chromium's reduced-motion emulation and with a 30 hour old pretend feed |
+| Google's spam policies list "Keyword-rich, hidden, or low-quality links embedded in widgets that are distributed across various sites" as link spam | developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies, section `#link-spam`, read 2026-10-07 (the page said it was last updated 2026-08-28). The gallery paraphrases it |
+| NASA's acknowledgement text for FIRMS (LANCE) | Quoted in full in `docs/hazard-sources.md` (FIRMS section); the gallery and the large widget print it word for word |
+| MET Norway's credit "The Norwegian Meteorological Institute, shortened MET Norway" and CC BY 4.0 | `docs/launch-licences.md` and `site/pages-sky.mjs` (`MET_CREDIT`); the licence page says NLOD 2.0 and CC BY 4.0 as recorded there |
+| The maps use Natural Earth coastlines, which are in the public domain | naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use, read 2026-10-07: "All versions of Natural Earth raster + vector map data found on this website are in the public domain" and "No permission is needed to use Natural Earth. Crediting the authors is unnecessary." `public/coast.bin` is made from world-atlas's `land-50m.json` (`docs/satcountry-sources.md`); github.com/topojson/world-atlas, read 2026-10-07, says it is "a convenient redistribution of Natural Earth's vector data, version 4.1.0" and carries the ISC licence |
+
+## Our choices (OURS, not from any source)
+- The coastline in each map widget: the app's coastlines simplified with Douglas-Peucker at 0.4 degrees on a half-degree grid, outlines under 2 degrees across left out (`COAST_EPS`, `COAST_MIN_SPAN`), about 6 KB of text.
+- The map box: 58 degrees south to 80 north, widened to take in every point of the data (`wLatBox`), so nothing in the feed is ever left off; the storm map is fitted around the storms and their tracks, with longitudes taken within 180 degrees of the strongest storm so a track across the date line stays in one piece.
+- The colours, sizes, animation speeds, the size presets (300 by 220, 400 by 300, 600 by 400, 800 by 450) and the allowed range (280 by 200 to 800 by 600).
+- The out-of-date rule: past the live page's limit the widget stops moving, keeps the old picture and says in a yellow line how old the data is; a failed load shows a plain message and no picture. The sky widget's chart is computed and always shows; only its cloud strip depends on the feed, and an out-of-date forecast is not drawn at all.
+
+## Licences and credits, per widget
+| Widget | Data | Terms as recorded | Credit shown |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Earthquakes | USGS | Public domain, credit requested (`docs/hazard-sources.md`, `test/fixtures/live-manifest.json`) | "Data: U.S. Geological Survey (USGS)" |
+| Aurora | NOAA SWPC Kp | The collector's record for the Kp file says "Not stated on the pages read" (`TERMS_VERIFIED.kp` is false in `site/hazard.mjs`). The NWS disclaimer (public domain unless noted) is recorded for SWPC's other products. NOT CONFIRMED that it covers the Kp file; the aurora live page already shows the same value | "Data: NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center" |
+| Tropical storms | NOAA NHC | NWS disclaimer, recorded and marked verified | "Data: NOAA National Hurricane Center" |
+| Wildfires | NASA FIRMS (LANCE) | "NASA supports full and open sharing of data", with its acknowledgement | "Data: NASA FIRMS (LANCE)." and, from 400 by 400, NASA's acknowledgement in full |
+| Tonight's sky | Computed; cloud from MET Norway | CC BY 4.0 with MET Norway's credit | "Moon and planets computed; cloud: MET Norway", and from 400 by 400 "(The Norwegian Meteorological Institute), CC BY 4.0" |
+Every widget ends with "Live data by Radar Around You", one link to the matching live page (`target="_blank" rel="noopener"`, so a click opens the page instead of replacing the frame).
+
+## The snippet and the credit link
+- One `iframe` (`src` on the site's own address from `SITE_URL`, `width`, `height`, a `title`, `loading="lazy"`, `referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"`, which is stricter than `no-referrer-when-downgrade` and needs nothing from the referrer, and `style="border:0;max-width:100%"`), no `allow` or `sandbox` attribute, plus one line under it: `Live data by <a href="...live page...">Radar Around You</a>`. The anchor text is the site's name, never keywords; nothing is hidden; no `rel` is forced on it, and the gallery says embedders may add `nofollow`.
+- Options go in the address (`?theme=light|dark`, `?city=` one of the six ids). `wOptions` takes only values on those lists (anything else falls back to the default), and the page uses the value only as one of those known words (the theme as an attribute value, the city to look up the coordinates and to finish the credit link), never as HTML. `e2e-embed.mjs` sends markup in both options and checks that it reaches neither the page nor the attribute.
+- `wSnippet` checks the widget, size, theme and city against the same lists and throws on anything else, and escapes the attributes. The gallery's generator runs the same function (copied by its source text) and builds its preview frame through DOM properties, never through HTML strings.
+
+## Framing
+- Nothing in the build blocks framing: no page sets `X-Frame-Options` or `frame-ancestors` (a meta tag cannot set `frame-ancestors` anyway), and the build writes no server configuration. The widget's meta content security policy limits what the widget itself loads, not who may frame it. `test/embed.test.js` checks the widget pages for either header name.
+- Seen on the live host on 2026-10-06 18:41 UTC (`curl -I https://zeninnov8.com/right-now/`, before this branch was deployed): no `X-Frame-Options`, and `content-security-policy: upgrade-insecure-requests` only, from `server: hcdn`. Hostinger generates the site's `.htaccess` and regenerates it on every redeploy (`docs/hosting.md`).
+- NOT CONFIRMED: that framing works on the live host after this is deployed. Check with `curl -sI https://zeninnov8.com/embed/earthquakes/` (look for `x-frame-options` and `frame-ancestors`) and a real iframe on another site. The build does not write `.htaccess`, so no cache rule for `/embed/` was added; if one is wanted it would have to be a Hostinger setting, which is the owner's call (not needed for the widgets to work).
+- The service worker (`public/sw.js`) treats a navigation as network first, so a first-party visit to `/embed/` always gets the current page; in a frame on another site the browser partitions it. NOT CONFIRMED in Safari.
+
+## Checked in a browser (e2e-embed.mjs)
+Each widget at 280 by 200, 400 by 300 and 800 by 600 in Chromium, served by a real local server with a pretend live folder (`test/embedpack.js`: the repository's feed fixtures moved to now): it loads, shows a summary with a number and a text alternative with the numbers, the credit link is visible inside the frame and points at the right page, the canvas is drawn, the page does not scroll, no request leaves the site, no console errors; the picture changes over time and holds still with reduced motion; `?theme=` overrides the system in both directions and no option follows it; unknown option values are ignored; with 30 hour old data it says out of date; with the live folder answering 503 it shows the plain message and no picture (the sky widget still shows its chart and says the cloud could not be loaded). The gallery: indexable, five plain-text snippets, the generator makes the snippet for a chosen widget, size, theme and city with a working preview; the snippet, pasted into a page served from another origin (a second local server on another port), frames the widget, which loads and renders its data, and the credit line shows under it without `nofollow`.
+
+## NOT CONFIRMED
+- Framing on the live host after deploy (above).
+- Whether Google counts the credit link at all, and how it treats it; whether the gallery is indexed or ranks.
+- Whether the short FIRMS credit in a small widget satisfies NASA's acknowledgement request (the full text shows from 400 by 400 and on the gallery, and the widget links to the wildfires page, which prints it in full).
+- The terms of NOAA's Kp file specifically (above).
+- Safari, Firefox and real phones (only Chromium was run); real host pages with strict content security policies.
+- The widget's accuracy for the sky is stated against astronomy-engine, not against an independent almanac; the sky pages' own USNO checks cover only astronomy-engine.
+
+## Known weaknesses
+- The widgets read the deploy's `live/` folder: right after a redeploy, until the pull job or the build-time snapshot restores it, they show the plain "could not be loaded" message (the sky chart still works).
+- The fire widget downloads `fires.bin` (about 200 KB on 4 October 2026) when its version changes; on a busy host page this is the heaviest widget.
+- The sky widget's planets are listed when above the horizon in a dark sky (Sun more than 6 degrees down), without the sky pages' magnitude rule, so a faint Mercury low in twilight can be named; the text says "above the horizon", not "visible".
+- At the smallest size the summary is shortened to one line (the full sentence stays in the text alternative), and the storm map labels only the strongest storm.
